<section class="story-section" id="problem">
  <p class="eyebrow">01 / The problem before</p>
  <h2>Why start from zero?</h2>
  <p>A <strong>label</strong> is an answer attached to an example: “positive” on a happy film review. Collecting answers takes work. Could reading first help?</p>
  <p>A <strong>language model</strong> learns to guess text from earlier clues. Researchers wanted to reuse what it had learned.</p>
</section>
<section class="story-section" id="idea">
  <p class="eyebrow">02 / The big idea</p>
  <h2>Read first. Specialise second.</h2>
  <p><strong>Pretraining</strong> is learning before a particular task. Predicting words supplies its own answers: the text tells you what comes next. <strong>Fine-tuning</strong> then updates the model using task examples. Think of reading widely before learning a new job.</p>
  <p>Three projects tried this. <strong>ELMo</strong> supplied context-sensitive representations: “bank” could look different beside “river” and “money.” <strong>ULMFiT</strong> adapted a model that processed words one at a time, first to the task’s unlabelled text, then to labels, changing its layers carefully.</p>
  <p><strong>GPT-1</strong> used a Transformer—a network where words exchange information through attention. It predicted the next chunk of text in books using earlier text, then adapted the network with a small task-specific output. The starting point was now an entire pretrained Transformer.</p>
