<section class="story-section" id="mattered">
  <p class="eyebrow">04 / Why it mattered</p>
  <h2>Reading became reusable.</h2>
  <p>Predicting language could prepare a model for other tasks. Pretraining learns from text; fine-tuning teaches a particular job.</p>
  <p>Next, BERT changed the reading exercise: a Transformer could use context on both sides of a missing word.</p>
  <a class="next-chapter" href="/chapters/bert/"><span>Next · 2018</span><strong>BERT ↗</strong><span>What can a missing word teach a model?</span></a>
</section>
<details class="chapter-disclosure" id="deeper">
  <summary>Go deeper <span>Objectives, transfer routes, results & limits</span></summary>
  <div class="disclosure-body">
    <h3>GPT-1: reuse and update the network</h3>
    <p>Its pretraining objective maximises <code>Σₜ log P(xₜ | x₁, …, xₜ₋₁; θ)</code>: give the observed next token high probability. A token is a chunk of text; θ denotes learned parameters. Fine-tuning maximises labelled-answer likelihood, usually with an additional weighted language-model objective. All pretrained layers are updated.</p>
    <p>The model has 12 Transformer decoder layers (which predict text using earlier positions), 768-dimensional states, and 12 attention heads (separate learned reads). It trained on BooksCorpus. Table 3 reports 86.5% Story Cloze accuracy versus 77.6% for the listed previous best. Story Cloze asks a model to choose a story’s ending. The study spans 12 English benchmarks; transfer does not establish general conversational ability.</p>
    <h3>ELMo: reuse features</h3>
    <p>Two recurrent networks, which process words one at a time, predict words from earlier or later context. Each position’s representation combines their internal states. During supervised task training, the language-model weights stay fixed; a task learns a weighted mixture of its layers, scaled by a learned number: <code>ELMoₖ = γ Σⱼ sⱼ hₖ,ⱼ</code>. The mixture weights s sum to one.</p>
    <p>Table 1 reports SQuAD question-answering test F1 of 85.8 for the baseline plus ELMo versus 81.1 for the baseline. F1 balances answer precision and recall. Reusing features still requires a task model and labelled training.</p>
    <h3>ULMFiT: adapt in stages</h3>
    <p>A three-layer LSTM—a recurrent network whose gates control what memory to keep and change—first learns general language, then adapts to unlabelled target text, then learns classification. Lower layers use smaller learning rates (update sizes); layers are gradually unfrozen (allowed to change). The learning rate rises briefly, then falls.</p>
    <p>Table 2 reports 4.6% test error on IMDb film-review ratings. That result averages separate forward and backward classifiers—models that assign categories. The experiments cover six English classification datasets. Transfer can fail when the reading and the task differ, and careless updates can erase useful earlier learning.</p>
    <h3>What the toy actually trains</h3>
    <p>Each adjective has one learned number e. A prediction is <code>sigmoid(w × e + b)</code>; sigmoid maps its input to a value between zero and one. Reading updates e, w, and b to predict “smile” versus “sigh.” Fine-tuning replaces the output with a fresh rating output, then updates it and the adjective numbers using the selected labels. Both stages use 100 passes over their tiny data.</p>
    <p>The comparison uses identical initial adjective numbers and identical label training. Unlabelled adjectives keep their reading patterns. This omits attention, word order, contextual representations, and realistic data; it illustrates transfer, not any paper’s implementation.</p>
  </div>
</details>
